8045,320,69,2075,96859,17,481,220,16,16,69,2075,8,488,220,18,15,284,856,61,19,481,220,16,19,87,61,18,488,220,21,17,87,61,17,481,220,24,16,87,488,220,19,17,13,1124,2533,1249,1477,17767,282,2075,8,1124,701,582,1184,311,8253,279,19703,315,279,47311,17767,856,61,19,481,220,16,19,87,61,18,488,220,21,17,87,61,17,481,220,24,16,87,488,220,19,17,1124,568,6771,594,78064,279,19703,315,419,47311,553,17767,435,62,16,11,435,62,17,11,435,62,18,11,435,62,19,1124,568,5005,11,582,614,510,78045,320,69,2075,8,481,435,62,16,2376,69,2075,8,481,435,62,17,2376,69,2075,8,481,435,62,18,2376,69,2075,8,481,435,62,19,8,284,856,61,19,481,220,16,19,87,61,18,488,220,21,17,87,61,17,481,220,24,16,87,488,220,19,17,13,1124,2533,12549,17767,342,2075,8,284,320,87,12,20,2376,87,12,21,8,1124,701,432,11017,429,17767,282,2075,8,1124,8,1969,387,825,315,279,19703,315,279,47311,17767,856,61,19,481,220,16,19,87,61,18,488,220,21,17,87,61,17,481,220,24,16,87,488,220,19,17,1124,8,28973,553,220,20,476,220,21,13,15277,11,279,3204,2750,315,17767,282,2075,8,1124,8,525,17767,435,62,16,11,435,62,17,11,435,62,18,11,435,62,19,11,435,62,16,10,20,11,435,62,17,10,20,11,435,62,18,10,20,11,435,62,19,10,20,11,435,62,16,10,21,11,435,62,17,10,21,11,435,62,18,10,21,11,435,62,19,10,21,1124,49555,522,37420,91339,29,151645,198,151644,872,198,2610,1231,312,18883,55856,279,3491,323,18130,279,6291,504,279,7167,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nLet $g(x) = x^2 - 11x + 30,$ and let $f(x)$ be a polynomial such that\n\\[g(f(x)) = x^4 - 14x^3 + 62x^2 - 91x + 42.\\]Find the sum of all possible values of $f(10^{100}).$\n\nPartial draft:\n<partial_draft>\nTo solve for the sum of all possible values of \\( f(10^{100}) \\), we start by analyzing the given equation \\( g(f(x)) = x^4 - 14x^3 + 62x^2 - 91x + 42 \\) where \\( g(x) = x^2 - 11x + 30 \\).\n\nFirst, let's express \\( g(f(x)) \\) in terms of \\( f(x) \\):\n\\[ g(f(x)) = (f(x))^2 - 11f(x) + 30. \\]\n\nWe know that:\n\\[ (f(x))^2 - 11f(x) + 30 = x^4 - 14x^3 + 62x^2 - 91x + 42. \\]\n\nTo find \\( f(x) \\), we need to determine the roots of the polynomial \\( x^4 - 14x^3 + 62x^2 - 91x + 42 \\). Let's denote the roots of this polynomial by \\( r_1, r_2, r_3, r_4 \\). Then, we have:\n\\[ (f(x) - r_1)(f(x) - r_2)(f(x) - r_3)(f(x) - r_4) = x^4 - 14x^3 + 62x^2 - 91x + 42. \\]\n\nSince \\( g(x) = (x-5)(x-6) \\), it follows that \\( f(x) \\) must be one of the roots of the polynomial \\( x^4 - 14x^3 + 62x^2 - 91x + 42 \\) shifted by 5 or 6. Therefore, the possible values of \\( f(x) \\) are \\( r_1, r_2, r_3, r_4, r_1+5, r_2+5, r_3+5, r_4+5, r_1+6, r_2+6, r_3+6, r_4+6 \\).\n\n\n</partial_draft>","role":"user"},{"content":"You may re-analyze the problem and rewrite the solution from the beginning.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"705039fef82f8d9dbe21ccf12f7e60c77d63fde41e891f1b18b992b4242f3661"},"seconds":39.43177938461304,"seed":1090503335320573777,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve for the sum of all possible values of \\( f(10^{100}) \\), we start by analyzing the given equation \\( g(f(x)) = x^4 - 14x^3 + 62x^2 - 91x + 42 \\) where \\( g(x) = x^2 - 11x + 30 \\).\n\nFirst, we factorize \\( g(x) \\):\n\\[ g(x) = (x-5)(x-6). \\]\n\nNext, we need to find the polynomial \\( f(x) \\) such that:\n\\[ g(f(x)) = (f(x)-5)(f(x)-6) = x^4 - 14x^3 + 62x^2 - 91x + 42. \\]\n\nTo find the roots of the polynomial \\( x^4 - 14x^3 + 62x^2 - 91x + 42 \\), we use the Rational Root Theorem which suggests that the possible rational roots are factors of the constant term (42) divided by the leading coefficient (1). Testing these possible roots, we find that \\( x = 1, 2, 3, 7 \\) are the roots. Thus, we can factorize the polynomial as:\n\\[ x^4 - 14x^3 + 62x^2 - 91x + 42 = (x-1)(x-2)(x-3)(x-7). \\]\n\nThis means that \\( f(x) \\) must be one of the roots 1, 2, 3, 7, or shifted by 5 or 6. Therefore, the possible values of \\( f(x) \\) are:\n\\[ 1, 2, 3, 7, 6, 7, 8, 12, 7, 8, 9, 13. \\]\n\nHowever, since \\( f(x) \\) must satisfy \\( g(f(x)) = (f(x)-5)(f(x)-6) \\), the possible values of \\( f(x) \\) are \\( 1, 2, 3, 7 \\) or \\( 6, 7, 8, 12 \\). The distinct possible values are \\( 1, 2, 3, 6, 7, 8, 12 \\).\n\nNow, we need to find the sum of all possible values of \\( f(10^{100}) \\):\n\\[ 1 + 2 + 3 + 6 + 7 + 8 + 12 = 39. \\]\n\nThus, the sum of all possible values of \\( f(10^{100}) \\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{39}. \\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{39}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To solve for the sum of all possible values of \\( f(10^{100}) \\), we start by analyzing the given equation \\( g(f(x)) = x^4 - 14x^3 + 62x^2 - 91x + 42 \\) where \\( g(x) = x^2 - 11x + 30 \\).\n\nFirst, we factorize \\( g(x) \\):\n\\[ g(x) = (x-5)(x-6). \\]\n\nNext, we need to find the polynomial \\( f(x) \\) such that:\n\\[ g(f(x)) = (f(x)-5)(f(x)-6) = x^4 - 14x^3 + 62x^2 - 91x + 42. \\]\n\nTo find the roots of the polynomial \\( x^4 - 14x^3 + 62x^2 - 91x + 42 \\), we use the Rational Root Theorem which suggests that the possible rational roots are factors of the constant term (42) divided by the leading coefficient (1). Testing these possible roots, we find that \\( x = 1, 2, 3, 7 \\) are the roots. Thus, we can factorize the polynomial as:\n\\[ x^4 - 14x^3 + 62x^2 - 91x + 42 = (x-1)(x-2)(x-3)(x-7). \\]\n\nThis means that \\( f(x) \\) must be one of the roots 1, 2, 3, 7, or shifted by 5 or 6. Therefore, the possible values of \\( f(x) \\) are:\n\\[ 1, 2, 3, 7, 6, 7, 8, 12, 7, 8, 9, 13. \\]\n\nHowever, since \\( f(x) \\) must satisfy \\( g(f(x)) = (f(x)-5)(f(x)-6) \\), the possible values of \\( f(x) \\) are \\( 1, 2, 3, 7 \\) or \\( 6, 7, 8, 12 \\). The distinct possible values are \\( 1, 2, 3, 6, 7, 8, 12 \\).\n\nNow, we need to find the sum of all possible values of \\( f(10^{100}) \\):\n\\[ 1 + 2 + 3 + 6 + 7 + 8 + 12 = 39. \\]\n\nThus, the sum of all possible values of \\( f(10^{100}) \\) is:\n\\[ \\boxed{39}. \\]","id":"intermediate_algebra:58"}
